{"answer":"Mechanical components of the Aircraft Fire Training Facility (AFTF) have a time‑dependent replacement schedule shown in Table 1.  Items such as LPG liquid piping hydrostatic relief valves, pneumatic system pilot valves, LPG flexible hoses, and LPG tank relief valves are to be replaced or rebuilt every 10 years (with a maximum operating window of 11 years)【P57】.  Electrical components, by contrast, are not subject to a replacement‑age schedule in this TO; instead they are inspected semi‑annually for corrosion, wear, or service‑limit conditions as part of the Semi‑Annual System Safety Inspection and Operational Checks【P234】.

Thus, mechanical parts are replaced at defined intervals, whereas electrical systems are maintained primarily through periodic inspection without an explicit replacement age.
","citations":["P57","P234"],"insufficient_evidence":false}

{"answer":"In the aircraft‑fuel‑servicing manual (TO 35‑1‑18), visual aids are primarily instructional—illustrations, tables and diagrams are distributed throughout the publication to supplement the text and show correct positioning of equipment, safety gear, and steps in the servicing sequence. These aids help operators visualise high‑risk operations (handling fuel, high‑pressure lines, ignition sources) so that the correct procedures are followed and potential hazards are identified, thereby reducing the chance of accidental ignition or spillage.

In contrast, the UID asset‑marking manual (TO 00‑25‑260) uses visual aids as inspection tools.  Magnifying loops and other optical magnification devices, along with digital scanning of the 2‑D data matrix, are employed to verify the physical condition and machine‑readability of existing UID labels.  The manual requires a minimum of 60 % readability (MIL‑STD‑130) and uses scanning to confirm that the encoded UII data match the item.  These aids ensure that tagging errors, corroded or damaged labels, or unreadable data are identified and corrected, preventing costly misidentification and ensuring asset integrity.

Thus, while the fuel‑servicing manual’s visual aids mainly support procedural comprehension and safety awareness, the UID manual’s visual aids focus on verification and quality control of identification marks in a high‑risk environment.","citations":["P428","P2294"],"insufficient_evidence":false}

{"answer":"CSO (Concurrent Servicing Operation) is the simultaneous fueling and/or refueling of an aircraft with other operations such as munitions loading/unloading, aircraft reconfiguration, aircraft –6 Technical Order inspections, and additional servicing like oil, nitrogen, and hydraulic fluid. The activities that are performed when a Chief Servicing Supervisor (CSS) is present include:

• Simultaneous fuel servicing with aircraft –6 and –6WC inspections;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with munitions/ammunition loading or unloading;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with aircraft reconfiguration;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with other aircraft servicing such as oil, nitrogen, and hydraulic fluid;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing that combines any of the above (loading/unloading of munitions, reconfiguration, –6 TO inspections, and other servicing).  In addition, the CSS is the on‑site supervisor responsible for controlling and monitoring all concurrent servicing operations and ensuring safety and compliance.","citations":["P570","P571"],"insufficient_evidence":false}

answer:
CSO (Concurrent Servicing Operation) is the simultaneous fueling and/or refueling of an aircraft with other operations such as munitions loading/unloading, aircraft reconfiguration, aircraft –6 Technical Order inspections, and additional servicing like oil, nitrogen, and hydraulic fluid. The activities that are performed when a Chief Servicing Supervisor (CSS) is present include:

• Simultaneous fuel servicing with aircraft –6 and –6WC inspections;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with munitions/ammunition loading or unloading;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with aircraft reconfiguration;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing with other aircraft servicing such as oil, nitrogen, and hydraulic fluid;
• Simultaneous fuel servicing that combines any of the above (loading/unloading of munitions, reconfiguration, –6 TO inspections, and other servicing).  In addition, the CSS is the on‑site supervisor responsible for controlling and monitoring all concurrent servicing operations and ensuring safety and compliance.
